\description{
Fits a regression model in which each subject contributes a whole array of
measurements -- an image, a connectivity matrix, a spectrogram, a
space-by-time grid -- and the outcome is a single number per subject, such
as a yes/no diagnosis, a count, or a continuous score. Ordinary regression
cannot be used directly here because one array holds far more numbers than
there are subjects. \code{spgtr()} solves that by compressing every dimension of
the array down to a handful of informative directions and fitting the
generalized linear model on those, then translating the answer back to the
original array shape so it can be read off like a picture.
}
\section{What you get back}{

The main output is a coefficient array of exactly the same shape as one
subject's data, available through \code{coef(fit)}. A large positive entry means
"a high value at this position pushes the outcome up"; a zero entry means
the position was not used. With \code{lambda > 0} whole rows, columns, or slices
are set to zero, so the fit also tells you which parts of the array matter
at all (see \code{fit$selected} and \code{\link[=summary.spgtr]{summary.spgtr()}}).
}

\section{How to use it (short version)}{

\enumerate{
\item Put your data in a list: \code{X[[i]]} is subject \code{i}'s matrix or array, all
the same shape. \code{y} is a vector with one entry per subject.
\item Run \code{fit <- spgtr(X, y)} for a yes/no outcome, or add
\code{family = poisson()} / \code{family = gaussian()} for counts / continuous
outcomes.
\item Look at \code{summary(fit)}, view \code{coef(fit)}, and predict new subjects with
\code{predict(fit, newX)}.
\item To also \emph{select} which parts of the array matter, use \code{\link[=spgtr_cv]{spgtr_cv()}},
which picks the amount of sparsity for you by cross-validation.
}
}

\section{How it works (technical)}{

The outcome enters through the working residual \code{r = y - mu_0}, where
\code{mu_0} is the fitted mean of the GLM of \code{y} on the nuisance covariates \code{Z}
alone (the intercept only, when \code{Z} is \code{NULL}). Writing \code{Sigma_k} for the
mode-\code{k} marginal covariance of the centered predictor and \code{C} for its
cross-covariance with \code{r}, the mode-\code{k} signal matrix is
\verb{U_k = C_(k) (kron_\{j != k\} Sigma_j^-1) C_(k)'}.

With \code{basis = "simpls"} the factor matrix \code{W_k} collects the first \code{u[k]}
SIMPLS directions of \verb{(U_k, Sigma_k)}, exactly as in \code{\link[=tepls]{tepls()}}. With
\code{basis = "envelope"} (the default) that basis is refined by minimizing the
envelope objective \verb{log|W' Sigma_k W| + log|W' (Sigma_k + U_k)^-1 W|} over
the Stiefel manifold. A positive \code{lambda} adds the adaptively weighted
row-wise penalty \verb{lambda * sum_i w_ki ||W_k[i, ]||_2}, whose proximal
operator zeroes entire rows of \code{W_k} and therefore drops the corresponding
mode-\code{k} slices of the predictor from the model. Weights \code{w_ki} are the
inverse row norms of the unpenalized SIMPLS basis.

The penalized problem is solved by the sequential linearized proximal
gradient method of Xiao, Liu & Yuan (2021) with alternating Barzilai-Borwein
step sizes. Its retraction is the polar factor \verb{W (W'W)^-1/2}, a
right-multiplication, which is what allows the zero rows created by the
proximal step to survive orthonormalization.

Finally the predictor is reduced to latent scores \verb{T_i = X_i x_1 W_1' ... x_m W_m'}, the GLM of \code{y} on \verb{(Z, vec(T))} is fit by \code{\link[stats:glm.fit]{stats::glm.fit()}}, and
the coefficient array is reconstructed as \verb{B = D x_1 W_1 ... x_m W_m} from
the latent coefficients \code{D}.
}

\section{Speed}{

The mode-wise covariances and the manifold solver are compiled kernels
(\code{spgtr_mode_covs_cpp}, \code{spgtr_slpg_cpp}); the score computation reuses the
package's compiled \code{\link[=ttm]{ttm()}}. Reference implementations in R are used
automatically if the package was installed without compilation, and the two
paths agree to numerical tolerance.
}

\examples{
# 120 subjects, each measured on an 8 x 6 grid; only the top-left corner
# of the grid actually drives the yes/no outcome.
set.seed(1)
B <- outer(c(1.5, rep(0, 7)), c(1.5, rep(0, 5)))
X <- lapply(1:120, function(i) matrix(rnorm(48), 8, 6))
eta <- vapply(X, function(xi) sum(B * xi), numeric(1))
y <- rbinom(120, 1, 1 / (1 + exp(-eta)))

fit <- spgtr(X, y, u = c(1, 1))
summary(fit)

# Coefficient array, same shape as one subject's data.
round(as.tensor(coef(fit))$as_array(), 2)

# Predicted probabilities and labels.
head(predict(fit, type = "response"))
head(predict(fit, type = "class"))

# With covariates, and with a count outcome.
Z <- cbind(age = rnorm(120))
fit_z <- spgtr(X, y, u = c(1, 1), Z = Z)
counts <- rpois(120, exp(eta / 2))
fit_p <- spgtr(X, counts, u = c(1, 1), family = poisson())
}
